#: manual/api/report.md:1
msgid "report API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:5
msgid "`Luna-Flow/mare_mark/report` turns a JSONL event stream into a Plot IR document and renders that document as `mmks_1` JSON, standalone SVG or a self-contained HTML page. All four functions are pure: they take strings and values and return strings. Reading and writing files is the job of the [`cli`](cli.md). The projection rules are explained in the [report design](../design/report.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:12
msgid "Source: [`src/report/report.mbt`](../../../src/report/report.mbt)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:25
msgid "The examples on this page call them through their default aliases (`@ir_model`, `@report`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:28
msgid "Parsing events"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:32
msgid "`document_from_jsonl` parses a JSONL event stream into a `PlotDocument`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:38
msgid "`target` (default `\"unknown\"`) is copied into the document; the events do not carry it. Blank lines are skipped. Each other line must be a JSON object, and its `artifact_version`, when present, must be the string `\"mmka_1\"`. Lines are interpreted by their `\"type\"`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:43
msgid "Required fields"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:45
msgid "`implementation` (string), `dataset_id`, `elapsed_us` (numbers)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:45
msgid "a plot point (x = `dataset_id`, y = `elapsed_us`, series = `implementation`), unless `valid` is `false` or `batch_sink` is not `\"kept\"`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:46
#: manual/api/report.md:47
#: manual/api/report.md:48
msgid "none"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:46
msgid "counted in the capability matrix by `implementation` and `actual_kind` (`\"unclassified\"` when absent); a status of `invalid` or `infrastructure_failure` adds a mismatch row and, when `case` and `dataset_id` are present, removes that implementation's points for that case and dataset"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:47
msgid "a counterexample row (minimal input, minimal fingerprint, shrink path, replay command)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:48
msgid "sets `run_id` and the corpus counts; the last summary wins"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:49
msgid "anything else"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:49
msgid "ignored, including `calibration`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:51
msgid "The document has one `Scaling` plot titled `\"Benchmark observations\"` with unit `\"µs/op\"` and interval kind `\"raw\"`, or no plot when no point survives. Exploratory and confirmatory observations are plotted together. The corpus total is the sum of the four summary counts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:56
msgid "Errors are messages with the 1-based line number: invalid JSON, a line that is not an object, an unsupported or non-string artifact version, an observation without `implementation`, `dataset_id` or `elapsed_us`, and a stream with neither points nor mismatches nor counterexamples."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:77
msgid "Rendering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:81
msgid "`plot_json` serializes a document as `mmks_1` JSON."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:87
msgid "The object has the keys `schema_version`, `run_id`, `target`, `plots` (each with `kind`, `title`, `unit`, `interval_kind` and `points` of `x`, `y`, `series`) and `differential` (`mismatches`, `capabilities`, `counterexamples`, `corpus`). Plot kinds are written in snake case (`scaling`, `raw_distribution`, `block_order`, `interval`, `outlier`, `change_point`, `heatmap`, `pareto`). The output is compact, and strings are escaped by the JSON encoder."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:108
msgid "`plot_svg` renders one plot as a standalone SVG element."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:114
msgid "The SVG has a 960 × 420 view box, an accessible `<title>` and `<desc>`, inline styles, five horizontal grid lines with tick values, at most about ten x labels, and a legend. Every point is drawn as a circle with a tooltip `series — x: y unit`. `Scaling`, `Interval` and `ChangePoint` plots also connect, per series, the mean of the points at each x category. `Heatmap` plots draw one cell per series and x category, with an opacity that grows with the mean value. An empty plot shows \"No observations\". All text is XML-escaped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:139
msgid "`html` renders a whole document as one self-contained HTML page."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:145
msgid "The page contains a header with run id, target and schema version, then the differential report (corpus outcomes, mismatches, capability coverage matrix, minimal counterexamples), then one figure per plot. CSS is inline, plots are inline SVG, and there are no scripts and no external resources, so the file can be archived or attached as it is. The output depends only on the document."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:1
msgid "runner API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:5
msgid "`Luna-Flow/mare_mark/runner` owns the measurement loop. You describe a benchmark case, compile it into a validated plan, and `run` it with a seed, an environment snapshot, an event sink and a validated protocol. The runner validates every implementation against an oracle before timing, calibrates the batch size, measures in balanced blocks and emits raw events. The reasons for each step are in the [runner design](../design/runner.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:12
msgid "Source: [`src/runner/runner.mbt`](../../../src/runner/runner.mbt), [`src/runner/bench_spec.mbt`](../../../src/runner/bench_spec.mbt)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:28
msgid "The examples on this page call them through their default aliases (`@model`, `@event`, `@runner`, `@async`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:31
msgid "Asynchrony and type parameters"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:33
msgid "`run` and `execute_operation` are `async`. Call them from an `async fn main` or an `async test`; the `moonbitlang/async` runtime that drives them is available on the native, JS and wasm targets, not on wasm-gc. Subprocess workers need the native target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:38
msgid "The type parameters recur throughout the package:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:40
msgid "Parameter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:42
msgid "the size parameter of a dataset, for example `Int`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:43
msgid "the value a fixture materializes for one dataset"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:44
msgid "the value an implementation runs on, produced by the fixture's `prepare`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:45
msgid "what the reference oracle computes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:46
msgid "what an implementation returns"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:47
msgid "state threaded from one operation to the next (`Unit` when stateless)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:48
msgid "accumulator and final value of the `OutputSink`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:50
msgid "Running a plan"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:54
msgid "`run` executes a compiled plan and returns the run summary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:60
msgid "For every scale, in order, the runner materializes the input once, validates every implementation against the oracle, warms up and calibrates every implementation, then measures `exploratory_samples` and `confirmatory_samples` blocks in balanced order. Every validation, failure, calibration and observation is sent to the sink as it happens; the summary is sent last, and the location string returned by the sink's `finish` becomes `artifact_location` of the returned summary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:68
msgid "The returned `RunSummary` has `complete == true`, the event counts, the validation tallies (`passed_count`, `failed_count`, `unsupported_count`, `expected_difference_count`) and the environment. Its `run_id` is `protocol_identity(protocol) + \":\" + case_id`; it identifies the protocol and case, not one execution, so put a unique id in the environment's provenance."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:74
msgid "A validation failure does not stop the measurement: the implementation is still timed and the failure is reported, so that a report can show the mismatch next to the series it invalidates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:114
msgid "With `QuickCheck` there is one exploratory and three confirmatory block per scale, so two scales and one implementation give eight observations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:119
msgid "`RunContext` carries everything a run needs besides the plan."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:131
msgid "Note the argument order of `RunContext::new`: environment, sink, seed, protocol. The seed is passed unchanged to the fixture through `GenerationContext.seed` and is mixed into the block order (see `balanced_order`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:138
msgid "`balanced_order` returns the cyclic rotation of implementation indices used for one block."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:145
msgid "`balanced_order(k, b)` is $[(0 + b) \\bmod k, (1 + b) \\bmod k, \\dots, (k-1+b) \\bmod k]$. Under `OrderPolicy::BalancedBlocks(order_seed)` the runner uses block offset $b + o$ with $o = (\\text{order\\_seed} \\oplus \\text{run\\_seed}) \\bmod k$; under `FixedOrder` it uses the identity. Over any $k$ consecutive blocks every implementation occupies every position exactly once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:159
msgid "Protocols"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:163
msgid "`ValidatedProtocol` is a `RunProtocol` that passed `validate_protocol`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:171
msgid "It can only be obtained from `validate_protocol` or a `ProtocolPreset`, so a plan never runs with a negative sample count or an empty iteration range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:176
msgid "`validate_protocol` checks a protocol and returns either a `ValidatedProtocol` or every violation found."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:183
msgid "Rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:183
msgid "Error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:186
msgid "`warmup_time_us`, when set, `>= 0`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:195
msgid "All rules are checked; the errors are returned together, in this order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:197
msgid "Each rule is implemented as a test for its violation (`x < 0.0`, `x <= 0.0`, `target > max`), and every comparison with `NaN` is false, so `NaN` passes all the `Double` rules. In particular a `NaN` `practical_delta_pct` is accepted and makes every `stats.compare_paired` decision on it `Equivalent` ([issue #1](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/mare_mark/issues/1)), a `NaN` `target_batch_time_us` disables calibration (the first batch is kept), and a `NaN` `warmup_time_us` leaves only the `warmup_iterations` count. Infinite durations pass as well, except a `target_batch_time_us` above a finite `max_sample_time_us`. A threshold of `0.0` is accepted although it reports exact ties as `Faster`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:232
msgid "`ProtocolConfigError` names one violated protocol rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:243
msgid "The `String` payload is the field name; the number is the rejected value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:247
msgid "`ProtocolPreset` names a ready-made protocol."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:259
msgid "`validated` returns the preset's protocol (for `Custom`, the wrapped one). The presets are:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:268
msgid "batch iterations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:268
msgid "1 to 1000"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:268
msgid "1 to 10000"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:268
msgid "5 to 10000"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:278
msgid "The runner acts on the warmup, calibration, order and sample fields. `experiment_design`, `outlier_policy`, `validation_coverage` and `practical_delta_pct` are recorded intent: validation always runs once per dataset before timing, and outlier filtering and decisions happen in `stats`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:283
msgid "Implementations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:287
msgid "`Implementation` is one competitor in a benchmark."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:299
msgid "`id` names it in events and must be unique and non-empty within a case; `version` is copied into every observation and validation. `initial_context` starts each validation sequence and each measured batch. `synchronize` is called immediately before the clock starts and after it stops; use it to wait for queued asynchronous work (a GPU stream, a thread pool)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:307
msgid "`Implementation::stateless` wraps a function of the prepared input that needs no context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:314
